#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# manipulate

#% text
# `manipulate(expr, [a, lo, hi], ...)` shows `expr` in the notebook with a slider for each parameter; moving a slider evaluates `expr` again with the parameter at the slider's value.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A control is `[a, lo, hi]` (continuous), `[a, lo, hi, step]` (in steps from `lo`) or `[[a, start], lo, hi]` / `[[a, start], lo, hi, step]` (starting at `start` instead of `lo`). The bounds, steps and starting values are numbers or constant expressions such as `2*pi`, `-1/2`, `sqrt(2)` or `10^-3`.

#% item
# With integer bounds and an integer step the parameter takes integer values; otherwise it takes machine reals.

#% item
# The notebook evaluates `{ let a = v; ...; expr }` for the sliders' values, so `expr` sees each parameter as a local name: a global value of the same name is not changed.

#% item
# Moving a slider evaluates while it moves: one evaluation runs at a time, positions reached meanwhile replace each other, and letting the slider go stops an evaluation of an older position.

#% item
# The positions are saved with the notebook, together with the last result, so a reopened notebook shows both without evaluating.

#% item
# The arguments are held: outside the notebook (in scripts and `mc-repl`) `manipulate(...)` stays as it is written.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A plot with a slider for the frequency (the notebook shows the slider and the plot):

#% cell
manipulate(plot(sin(a*x), [x, 0, 2*pi]), [a, 1, 5])
#% output
#= manipulate(plot(sin(a*x), [x, 0, 2*pi]), [a, 1, 5])

#% text
# Two parameters, one in steps of 1 starting at 3:

#% cell
manipulate(expand((x + b)^n), [[n, 3], 1, 8, 1], [b, -2, 2])
#% output
#= manipulate(expand((x + b)^n), [[n, 3], 1, 8, 1], [b, -2, 2])

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The expression is evaluated as `{ let ...; expr }`: a global value of the parameter's name is left alone:

#% cell
a = 10;
{ let a = 2.5; a^2 }
#% output
#= 6.25

#% cell
a
#% output
#= 10

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `plot`, `plot3d`, `table`
